---
name: std-mcp-client
version: 0.1.0
description: MCP client primitives — mcpConnect + McpClient. Wraps @modelcontextprotocol/sdk on JS and io.modelcontextprotocol:sdk on JVM. Not available on interpreter or scalajs-spa.
package: std.mcp
exports:
  # As in server.ssc: re-export the names imported from ./types.ssc, so a client consumer does
  # not have to import the same API from two modules. `Transport` is the one that was measured
  # missing (mcp-client-invoke), the rest are its siblings in the same import.
  - McpClient
  - ToolDescriptor
  - ResourceDescriptor
  - PromptDescriptor
  - ArgSpec
  - mcpConnect
  - Transport
  - Content
  - ToolResult
  - ResourceResult
  - PromptResult
  - McpError
---

# `std.mcp.client`

MCP client API. `mcpConnect(transport)` returns an `McpClient`; call `.close()` when done.

Requires `Feature.McpClient` — available on `jvm` and `js` (Node) backends only.

```scalascript
[ToolResult, ResourceResult, PromptResult, Content, Transport, McpError](./types.ssc)

// ─── Descriptor types (what the server advertised) ───────────────────────────

case class ArgSpec(name: String, typeName: String, required: Boolean)

case class ToolDescriptor(name: String, description: String, schema: Map[String, Any])

case class ResourceDescriptor(uri: String, name: String, mimeType: String)

case class PromptDescriptor(name: String, description: String, args: List[ArgSpec])

// ─── McpClient trait ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

extern class McpClient:
  // Discovery
  def listTools(): List[ToolDescriptor]
  def listResources(): List[ResourceDescriptor]
  def listPrompts(): List[PromptDescriptor]

  // Invocation — synchronous variants
  def callTool(name: String, args: Map[String, Any]): ToolResult
  def readResource(uri: String): ResourceResult
  def getPrompt(name: String, args: Map[String, Any]): PromptResult

  // Lifecycle
  def close(): Unit
  def isClosed: Boolean

  // ── Primitive surface ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // The same operations in types a RUNTIME can name. The members above traffic in
  // `ToolDescriptor`, `ToolResult` and `Transport`, which are GENERATED types — they exist only
  // once a program reaches them, so a backend runtime cannot declare a signature mentioning one.
  // That is the same boundary `route` hit on the HTTP lane, where the `Request` literal has to be
  // built in generated code.
  //
  // These take and return strings, lists of strings and booleans only, so a backend can implement
  // them directly. `callToolText` returns the concatenated text content, which is what an agent
  // driving a tool actually consumes; a caller needing the full structured `ToolResult` uses
  // `callTool` above on a lane that has an SDK behind it.
  def listToolNames(): List[String]
  def callToolText(name: String, argsJson: String): String
  def readResourceText(uri: String): String
  def isOpen(): Boolean

// ─── Factory ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Connect to an MCP server on the given transport.
// Blocks until the handshake completes; throws McpError on failure.
extern def mcpConnect(transport: Transport): McpClient
extern def mcpConnect(transport: Transport, timeoutMs: Long): McpClient

// Spawn a server process and speak JSON-RPC over its stdio. The primitive counterpart of
// `mcpConnect(Transport.Spawn(...))`: same transport, but the arguments are a command and its
// argv rather than a generated enum, so a backend runtime can implement it.
extern def mcpConnectSpawn(command: String, args: List[String]): McpClient
```
